\begin{didyouknow}{Weather by Hand}
Lewis Fry Richardson's 1922 book \emph{Weather Prediction by Numerical Process} replaced the equations of the atmosphere with finite differences on a grid. His hand-computed six-hour forecast came out badly wrong, largely because of problems with the initial data. He imagined a forecast factory of 64{,}000 human computers. Electronic computers later made his grid approach the basis of modern forecasting. He also invented Richardson extrapolation, which you meet in this chapter.
\end{didyouknow}

