\begin{didyouknow}{Kepler and the Wine Barrels}
In 1615 Johannes Kepler published a book on measuring the volume of wine barrels, prompted by buying wine for his 1613 wedding and doubting the merchants' measuring method. One of his formulas is equivalent to what we now call Simpson's rule, which Thomas Simpson published in 1743. Grid integration is old: weight a few sample values cleverly and you get an excellent estimate of an area.
\end{didyouknow}

Put $V=V_0+\varepsilon$ with small $\varepsilon$ in Eq.~(2). Then $\sqrt{V-V_0}=\sqrt\varepsilon$, $\sqrt V\approx\sqrt{V_0}$, and the second logarithm is $\log\big[(1+\sqrt{\varepsilon/V_0})^2\big]\approx2\sqrt{\varepsilon/V_0}$, so
\begin{equation*}
x\approx\frac1\pi\left[\sqrt\varepsilon\log\frac{V_0}{2\pi e^2}+2\sqrt\varepsilon\right]=\frac{\sqrt\varepsilon}{\pi}\log\frac{V_0}{2\pi}.
\end{equation*}
Solving for $\varepsilon$, $V\approx V_0+\big(\pi/\log(V_0/2\pi)\big)^2x^2\approx V_0+51.3\,x^2$: a smooth parabola, no kink at $x=0$.

