Put $V=V_0+\eta^2$ with $\eta=\sqrt{V-V_0}\ge0$ (a new symbol, unrelated to machine epsilon $\epsilon$ and to the perturbation parameter $\varepsilon$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:finite-differences-hf}). First rationalize the argument of the second logarithm by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt V+\eta$:
\begin{equation*}
\frac{\sqrt V+\eta}{\sqrt V-\eta}=\frac{(\sqrt V+\eta)^2}{V-\eta^2}=\frac{(\sqrt V+\eta)^2}{V_0},
\end{equation*}
so Eq.~(2) reads
\begin{equation*}
x=\frac1\pi\left[\eta\log\frac{V_0}{2\pi e^2}+2\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}\,\log\frac{\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}+\eta}{\sqrt{V_0}}\right].
\end{equation*}
Now let $\eta$ be small. Then $\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}\approx\sqrt{V_0}$, and with $\log(1+u)\approx u$ for small $u$, $\log\big[(\sqrt{V_0}+\eta)/\sqrt{V_0}\big]=\log(1+\eta/\sqrt{V_0})\approx\eta/\sqrt{V_0}$, so
\begin{equation*}
x\approx\frac1\pi\left[\eta\log\frac{V_0}{2\pi e^2}+2\eta\right]=\frac{\eta}{\pi}\log\frac{V_0}{2\pi}\qquad\text{(since }\log e^2=2\text{)}.
\end{equation*}
Solving for $\eta^2=V-V_0$, $V\approx V_0+\big(\pi/\log(V_0/2\pi)\big)^2x^2\approx V_0+51.3\,x^2$. This leading-order parabola does not by itself exclude higher terms such as $\abs{x}^3$, which would be a hidden kink. To exclude them, read the formula for $x$ as a function of $\eta$ allowed to take either sign. The factor $\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}$ is even in $\eta$. The logarithm is odd in $\eta$, because $(\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}-\eta)(\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}+\eta)=V_0$ gives $\log\big[(\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}-\eta)/\sqrt{V_0}\big]=-\log\big[(\sqrt{V_0+\eta^2}+\eta)/\sqrt{V_0}\big]$. The first term is odd as well. So $x(\eta)$ is an odd smooth function with nonzero slope $\log(V_0/2\pi)/\pi$ at $\eta=0$ (here $V_0/2\pi=1.550$, whose logarithm is positive). A smooth function with nonzero slope has a smooth inverse (first-year calculus), so $\eta$ is a smooth odd function of $x$, and $V=V_0+\eta^2$ is a smooth even function of $x$: no kink at $x=0$.

