\begin{didyouknow}{Surprise in the Simulation}
In 1955 Enrico Fermi, John Pasta, Stanislaw Ulam and Mary Tsingou, who programmed the computer, simulated a chain of masses joined by slightly nonlinear springs on the MANIAC at Los Alamos. They expected the energy to spread evenly among all modes. Instead it sloshed among a few modes and almost returned to its starting state. It was among the first discoveries made by numerical experiment.
\end{didyouknow}
